\newlabel{fig:system}{{1}{3}{\textbf {\sys {} architecture.} (1)~$\sigma _u$ holds one read-only trunk; \texttt {fork} aliases it in $O(1)$; LCP binds the winner. (2)~A residual exits at the first decisive action. $m_i=\ell _i$ is $\mathrm {HS}$ ($\Work {=}0$); $0{<}m_i{<}\ell _i$ is $\mathrm {HP}$; a repeated loop is $\mathrm {DS}$; the first $\alpha \ell _i$ failing is $\mathrm {EA}$; otherwise $\mathrm {PF}$ prefills $\ell _i$ on the alias, and a low score that is not a loop is probed for $t_0$ tokens before it may continue. Index $0$ stays in $\Keep $, and $\Xfer $ sums $\Work _i$ only over that kept set. (3)~Slots are $C=\lfloor H/M_{\mathrm {spine}}\rfloor $; $B^c_t$ runs beside $B^s_t$, and a known suffix is pinned when $p{=}1$ and $\Pref \le \Idle $. After abort the live occupancy is the spine; the same pool admits $76{\to }103$ sessions}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:system@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][2][]3}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{tab:trad}{{1}{3}{Where each traditional method pays for one fan-out of $k$ residuals on a trunk of length $L$. ``First expand'' is the GPU prefill issued before any child has published a block. ``Rejection'' is the earliest point at which a non-winner can leave. \sys is the stage order of \S \ref {sec:app}}{table.1}{}}
